course: dictionary
lesson: 10448
title: The sneaky trumpetfish
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A trumpetfish is a long, thin fish with a snout shaped like a trumpet. The snout is the front part of a fish's head, where its mouth is. Trumpetfish live on warm coral reefs.
explain: Read the clues in this sentence: «The trumpetfish hides by swimming glued to the side of a larger harmless fish.» Hides and glued tell you it is trying not to be seen. Harmless tells you the bigger fish is no danger to it, so the trumpetfish can stay close.
explain: Now read on: «...it also hangs head down among soft corals looking like one more stick.» Looking like a stick means it blends in with its surroundings. Hiding in plain sight like this is called camouflage, and it helps a hunter get close to its food without being spotted.
fact: A trumpetfish is a long, thin fish with a tube-shaped snout like a trumpet.
fact: Trumpetfish live on warm coral reefs.
fact: A trumpetfish often hangs head-down among sea plants and corals.
fact: It hides behind bigger fish so that small fish and shrimps do not notice it coming.
fact: When it is close enough, it sucks its prey into its snout, a bit like sipping a drink through a straw.
exercise: «The trumpetfish hides by swimming glued to the side of a larger harmless fish.» Why does it hide this way? | *To sneak up on small fish without being seen | To keep warm in the cold sea | To win a race against the bigger fish | To ask the bigger fish for help
exercise: What is a trumpetfish's snout shaped like? | A bicycle pump | *A trumpet | A paintbrush | A spoon
exercise: «...hangs head down among soft corals looking like one more stick.» What does looking like a stick help the trumpetfish do? | *Blend in and stay hidden | Swim much faster | Make a loud noise | Attract a mate
test: What do trumpetfish suck into their snouts? | Sea plants | Warm water | *Small fish and shrimps | Coral reefs
test: Where does a trumpetfish live? | In freshwater rivers | On a sandy desert | *On warm coral reefs
exam: Why is hiding behind a bigger fish a clever trick for a trumpetfish? | *Because small fish do not notice it coming until it is close | Because it helps the trumpetfish sleep | Because it makes the trumpetfish too big to see | Because the bigger fish always chases it away
